{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Plan review on this ticket took three rounds, and every finding was correct. Recording them\nbecause two would have been silent failures, not loud ones.\n\nROUND 1, finding A (G1G2): I put the \"re-probe the lock between tickets\" work in\n`compact_trigger.py`. The per-ticket loop is not there — it is `compact_all_cli`'s loop in\n`compact.py:386`. `run_sweep` only probes once before calling it. Wrong file for the fix.\n\nROUND 1, finding B (G6): THE IMPORTANT ONE. I named\n`authorship.build_introducing_commit_map` as the one-pass replacement. That builder is keyed by\nevent PATH, but `_build_authorship_ledger` looks up `position_commits.get(position_str)`\n(`compact_txn.py:127`) — by POSITION. A path-keyed map misses EVERY lookup, so every event\nfalls through to the per-event `resolve_event_commit`, silently re-introducing the exact\nper-event walks this ticket exists to remove. The output would have been correct and the\nperformance unchanged, with nothing to notice. The right primitive is the position-keyed\nsibling `build_position_commit_map` (`attest/authorship.py:466`), which I verified reads\nposition keys, walks history once, and falls back per-entry fail-closed.\n\nThat finding also changed a test: my \"one walk per sweep\" criterion counted the per-sweep\nBUILDER only, which a key mismatch would still satisfy. It now counts the per-event resolvers\ntoo, so a silent fallback fails the test instead of passing on identical output. A ledger-parity\ntest alone could never have caught this.\n\nROUND 2 (G1G2): `_build_authorship_ledger` has a second consumer I had not enumerated —\n`compact_rebuild.py:160`, the fsck SNAPSHOT-rebuild path — plus six existing test call sites\npassing `(paths, None)` positionally. Confirmed by grep rather than taken on faith. So the new\nmap parameter is OPTIONAL (keyword, default None): the rebuild path folds ONE ticket, where a\nper-ticket walk is already the right cost, and it stays untouched. Added an acceptance criterion\nthat calling without the argument yields the same ledger.\n\nThe pattern across all three: I was reaching for a plausible-looking primitive and a plausible\nscope without checking the key shape or the consumer set. The reviewer checked both. Worth\nnoting that the two findings a passing test suite would NOT have caught are exactly the two I\nwould have shipped."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786649043620909001,"uuid":"3f2cd341-a8b9-40d4-a313-45a124ae2a2d"}